text:
1.2 Counting the modes (slides 5–6)
The panel draws a two-dimensional slice of mode space (\(n_z=1\)) and the shell between \(p\) and \(p+dp\). The table counts the modes of the full 3D box by brute force and compares with the continuum formula \(\tfrac{\pi}{6}p^3\) (one polarisation).


Show the Python
viewof mc = Inputs.form({
  p: Inputs.range([4, 40], {value: 10, step: 0.5, label: "shell radius p = 2Lν/c"}),
  dp: Inputs.range([0.5, 5], {value: 1.5, step: 0.1, label: "shell thickness dp"})
})









Show the Python
mcOut = {
  const pts = [], R = mc.p + mc.dp, N = Math.ceil(R) + 1;
  for (let i = 1; i &lt;= N; i++) for (let j = 1; j &lt;= N; j++) {
    const r = Math.hypot(i, j); pts.push({nx: i, ny: j, inShell: r &gt;= mc.p && r &lt; mc.p + mc.dp, inside: r &lt; mc.p});
  }
  let count = 0, shell = 0;                                   // brute-force 3D count
  for (let i = 1; i &lt;= N; i++) for (let j = 1; j &lt;= N; j++) for (let k = 1; k &lt;= N; k++) {
    const r2 = i*i + j*j + k*k; if (r2 &lt; mc.p*mc.p) count++; else if (r2 &lt; R*R) shell++;
  }
  const arc = d3.range(0, 91).map(a =&gt; ({x: mc.p*Math.cos(a*Math.PI/180), y: mc.p*Math.sin(a*Math.PI/180)}));
  const arc2 = arc.map(d =&gt; ({x: d.x*R/mc.p, y: d.y*R/mc.p}));
  return {pts, count, shell, arc, arc2, N};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.min(width, 520), height: Math.min(width, 520), x: {label: "n_x", domain: [0, mcOut.N]}, y: {label: "n_y", domain: [0, mcOut.N]},
  marks: [
    Plot.dot(mcOut.pts, {x: "nx", y: "ny", r: mcOut.N &gt; 30 ? 1.5 : 2.5, fill: d =&gt; d.inShell ? "#eb6834" : d.inside ? "#2a78d6" : "#c3c2b7"}),
    Plot.line(mcOut.arc, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "#0b0b0b"}), Plot.line(mcOut.arc2, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "#0b0b0b", strokeDasharray: "4,3"})
  ]
})


















Show the Python
html`&lt;div class="result-box"&gt;3D box, one polarisation: &lt;b&gt;${mcOut.count}&lt;/b&gt; modes with p &lt; ${mc.p} (continuum π p³/6 = ${(Math.PI/6*mc.p**3).toFixed(0)},
ratio ${(mcOut.count/(Math.PI/6*mc.p**3)).toFixed(3)}) · &lt;b&gt;${mcOut.shell}&lt;/b&gt; modes in the shell (continuum (π/2) p² dp ≈ ${(Math.PI/2*mc.p**2*mc.dp + Math.PI/2*mc.p*mc.dp**2 + Math.PI/6*mc.dp**3).toFixed(0)})&lt;/div&gt;`







For small \(p\) the brute-force count falls below the continuum value. The points on the three planes \(n_i=0\) are not modes (the field vanishes), and the sphere “loses” a surface layer. The relative error falls as \(1/p\). For visible light in a 1 cm box, \(p=2L\nu/c\approx4\times10^4\), so the continuum formula is exact for all practical purposes.


Show the Python
p = np.unique(np.round(np.logspace(0.7, 2.2, 25)))
ratio = [lz.count_modes_box(1.0, P*lz.c0/2)/(np.pi/6*P**3) for P in p]
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 3.3))
ax.semilogx(p, ratio, "o-", ms=4, label="brute force / continuum")
ax.semilogx(p, 1 - 9/(4*p), "--", color=lz.INK2, lw=1.2, label="1 − 9/(4p) (surface correction)")
ax.set(xlabel="p = 2Lν/c", ylabel="ratio", ylim=(0.5, 1.05)); ax.legend(fontsize=8); plt.show()








Figure 1.1: Brute-force count of the modes of a cubic box (one polarisation) divided by the continuum value π p³/6. The ratio tends to 1 as 1 − 9/(4p): the three planes n_i = 0 carry no modes, a surface correction that vanishes for large p.

1.3 The density of modes and the ultraviolet catastrophe (slide 7)


Show the Python
viewof bb = Inputs.form({
  T: Inputs.range([200, 10000], {value: 3000, step: 50, label: "temperature T (K)"}),
  scale: Inputs.radio(["linear", "log"], {value: "log", label: "axes"})
})









Show the Python
bbOut = {
  const h = 6.62607015e-34, c = 299792458, k = 1.380649e-23, T = bb.T, data = [];
  for (let i = 0; i &lt;= 400; i++) {
    const lam = 10 ** (-7 + 3*i/400), nu = c/lam;                               // 100 nm .. 100 µm
    const M = 2*Math.PI*h*c*c/lam**5/Math.expm1(h*c/(lam*k*T))*1e-6;             // W m^-2 µm^-1
    const MRJ = 2*Math.PI*c*k*T/lam**4*1e-6;
    data.push({lam: lam*1e6, M, type: "Planck"}, {lam: lam*1e6, M: MRJ, type: "Rayleigh–Jeans"});
  }
  const peak = 2.897771955e-3/T*1e6, total = 5.670374419e-8*T**4;
  return {data, peak, total};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.min(width, 1050), height: 330, x: {label: "wavelength (µm)", type: bb.scale, domain: [0.1, 100]},
  y: {label: "spectral emittance M_λ (W m⁻² µm⁻¹)", type: bb.scale, grid: true,
      domain: bb.scale === "log" ? [1e-2, 1e9] : [0, 1.3*Math.max(...bbOut.data.filter(d =&gt; d.type === "Planck").map(d =&gt; d.M))]},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["Planck", "Rayleigh–Jeans"], range: ["#2a78d6", "#e34948"]},
  marks: [Plot.line(bbOut.data.filter(d =&gt; d.M &gt; 1e-3 && d.M &lt; 1e10), {x: "lam", y: "M", stroke: "type", strokeWidth: 2, clip: true}),
          Plot.ruleX([bbOut.peak], {stroke: "#52514e", strokeDasharray: "3,3"}),
          Plot.rectX([{x1: 0.38, x2: 0.78}], {x1: "x1", x2: "x2", fill: "#eda100", fillOpacity: 0.12})]
})


















Show the Python
html`&lt;div class="result-box"&gt;Wien peak λ&lt;sub&gt;max&lt;/sub&gt; = 2898/T = &lt;b&gt;${bbOut.peak.toFixed(3)} µm&lt;/b&gt; · total emittance σT⁴ = &lt;b&gt;${bbOut.total.toExponential(3)} W m⁻²&lt;/b&gt;
(the Rayleigh–Jeans integral diverges) · shaded: visible range&lt;/div&gt;`









Show the Python
wl = np.logspace(-7, np.log10(3e-4), 600)
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7.5, 4))
for T, c in zip((8000, 1200, 300), lz.PALETTE):
    ax.loglog(wl*1e6, lz.planck_emittance(wl, T)*1e-6, color=c, label=f"T = {T} K")
    ax.loglog(wl*1e6, 2*np.pi*lz.c0*lz.kB*T/wl**4*1e-6, "--", color=c, lw=1)
ax.set(xlabel="wavelength (µm)", ylabel="emittance (W m⁻² µm⁻¹)", ylim=(1e-2, 1e9), xlim=(0.1, 300)); ax.legend(fontsize=8); plt.show()








Figure 1.2: Black-body spectral emittance at the three temperatures of slide 7 (solid) and the Rayleigh–Jeans law (dashed). The classical law agrees only at long wavelengths, where hν ≪ k_BT.

2.2 Why two levels cannot be inverted
Pump a two-level system optically, as hard as you like. Absorption takes atoms up, but the same photons stimulate them down with the same coefficient \(B\). With \(W=B\rho\) the stimulated rate per atom,
\[
\frac{dN_2}{dt}=W(N_1-N_2)-\frac{N_2}{\tau_{sp}}\ \Rightarrow\ \frac{N_2}{N_0}\Big|_\text{steady}=\frac{W\tau_{sp}}{1+2W\tau_{sp}}&lt;\frac12.
\]


Show the Python
viewof tl = Inputs.form({
  s: Inputs.range([-2, 3], {value: 0, step: 0.05, label: "log₁₀ (W τ_sp): pump strength"}),
  tmax: Inputs.range([1, 10], {value: 5, step: 0.5, label: "time window (units of τ_sp)"})
})









Show the Python
tlOut = {
  const s = 10 ** tl.s, data = [];
  for (let i = 0; i &lt;= 300; i++) {
    const t = tl.tmax*i/300, ss = s/(1 + 2*s), N2 = ss*(1 - Math.exp(-(1 + 2*s)*t));
    data.push({t, f: N2, who: "N₂/N₀"}, {t, f: 1 - N2, who: "N₁/N₀"});
  }
  return {data, ss: s/(1 + 2*s), s};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.min(width, 1050), height: 280, x: {label: "time t/τ_sp"}, y: {label: "population fraction", domain: [0, 1], grid: true},
  color: {legend: true, range: ["#2a78d6", "#eb6834"]},
  marks: [Plot.line(tlOut.data, {x: "t", y: "f", stroke: "who", strokeWidth: 2}), Plot.ruleY([0.5], {stroke: "#e34948", strokeDasharray: "5,3"})]
})


















Show the Python
html`&lt;div class="result-box"&gt;steady state: N₂/N₀ = &lt;b&gt;${tlOut.ss.toFixed(4)}&lt;/b&gt;, N₂/N₁ = &lt;b&gt;${(tlOut.ss/(1 - tlOut.ss)).toFixed(4)}&lt;/b&gt; &lt; 1 ·
the medium ${tlOut.ss &gt; 0.45 ? "is &lt;b&gt;saturated&lt;/b&gt; (transparent) but never amplifies" : "still absorbs"}&lt;/div&gt;`







At most the medium becomes transparent (\(N_2\to N_1\)). This is why the ruby laser needs a third level and why the semiconductor laser needs electrical injection (Chapter 3, Chapter 9).

3.2 Inversion versus pump rate


Show the Python
viewof lv = Inputs.form({
  lw: Inputs.range([-3, 2], {value: 0.3, step: 0.02, label: "log₁₀ (W_p τ): pump rate × upper-level lifetime"}),
  r10: Inputs.range([-6, -1], {value: -4, step: 0.1, label: "4-level: log₁₀ (τ₁₀/τ₂₁), lower-level emptying"}),
  dth: Inputs.range([0.001, 0.2], {value: 0.02, step: 0.001, label: "threshold inversion ΔN_th/N₀"})
})









Show the Python
lvOut = {
  const r = 10 ** lv.r10, three = x =&gt; (x - 1)/(x + 1), four = x =&gt; x*(1 - r)/(1 + x*(1 + r)), data = [];
  for (let i = 0; i &lt;= 300; i++) { const x = 10 ** (-3 + 5*i/300); data.push({x, d: three(x), who: "3-level (ruby)"}, {x, d: four(x), who: "4-level (Nd:YAG)"}); }
  const x = 10 ** lv.lw;
  const need3 = (1 + lv.dth)/(1 - lv.dth), need4 = lv.dth/((1 - r) - lv.dth*(1 + r));
  return {data, x, d3: three(x), d4: four(x), need3, need4};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.min(width, 1050), height: 320, x: {label: "W_p τ", type: "log"}, y: {label: "inversion (N₂ − N₁)/N₀", domain: [-1, 1], grid: true},
  color: {legend: true, range: ["#e34948", "#2a78d6"]},
  marks: [Plot.line(lvOut.data, {x: "x", y: "d", stroke: "who", strokeWidth: 2}), Plot.ruleY([0], {stroke: "#0b0b0b"}),
          Plot.ruleY([lv.dth], {stroke: "#1baf7a", strokeDasharray: "5,3"}), Plot.ruleX([lvOut.x], {stroke: "#52514e", strokeDasharray: "2,3"}),
          Plot.dot([{x: lvOut.x, y: lvOut.d3}, {x: lvOut.x, y: lvOut.d4}], {x: "x", y: "y", r: 5, fill: ["#e34948", "#2a78d6"]})]
})


















Show the Python
html`&lt;div class="result-box"&gt;at W&lt;sub&gt;p&lt;/sub&gt;τ = ${lvOut.x.toFixed(3)}: 3-level inversion = &lt;b&gt;${lvOut.d3.toFixed(3)}&lt;/b&gt;, 4-level inversion = &lt;b&gt;${lvOut.d4.toFixed(4)}&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;
pump needed for ΔN&lt;sub&gt;th&lt;/sub&gt;/N₀ = ${lv.dth}: 3-level W&lt;sub&gt;p&lt;/sub&gt;τ = &lt;b&gt;${lvOut.need3.toFixed(3)}&lt;/b&gt;, 4-level W&lt;sub&gt;p&lt;/sub&gt;τ = &lt;b&gt;${lvOut.need4.toExponential(2)}&lt;/b&gt;
→ the 3-level laser needs &lt;b&gt;${(lvOut.need3/lvOut.need4).toFixed(0)}×&lt;/b&gt; more pump&lt;/div&gt;`







The four-level curve starts at zero and rises linearly: any pump gives gain. The three-level curve starts at \(-1\) (full absorption) and crosses zero only at \(W_p\tau=1\). For a typical threshold inversion of a few percent, the four-level laser needs tens of times less pump. That is why the ruby laser runs only in pulses while Nd:YAG runs continuously (slide 14 notes).

5.2 Gain curve and cavity modes (slides 48–50)
The panel draws the Doppler gain curve and the cavity modes. Modes whose gain exceeds the loss line (threshold, Chapter 6) can lase.


Show the Python
gasLines = ({"He–Ne 632.8 nm (Ne, 20.18 g/mol)": [632.8, 20.18], "He–Ne 543.5 nm (green)": [543.5, 20.18],
             "He–Ne 1523 nm (IR)": [1523.1, 20.18], "Ar⁺ 488.0 nm (39.95 g/mol)": [488.0, 39.95],
             "Ar⁺ 514.5 nm": [514.5, 39.95], "CO₂ 10.6 µm (44 g/mol)": [10600, 44.0]})
viewof gl = Inputs.form({
  line: Inputs.select(Object.keys(gasLines), {value: "He–Ne 632.8 nm (Ne, 20.18 g/mol)", label: "laser line"}),
  T: Inputs.range([200, 2000], {value: 400, step: 10, label: "gas temperature T (K)"}),
  L: Inputs.range([5, 200], {value: 40, step: 1, label: "cavity length L (cm)"}),
  thr: Inputs.range([0, 0.99], {value: 0.5, step: 0.01, label: "threshold / peak gain"}),
  shift: Inputs.range([0, 1], {value: 0, step: 0.01, label: "mode position (fraction of Δν_m)"})
})


















Show the Python
glOut = {
  const [lam0, Mg] = gasLines[gl.line], c = 299792458, k = 1.380649e-23, M = Mg*1e-3/6.02214076e23;
  const nu0 = c/(lam0*1e-9), fw = 2*nu0*Math.sqrt(2*k*gl.T*Math.log(2)/(M*c*c)), dnm = c/(2*gl.L*1e-2);
  const G = f =&gt; Math.exp(-4*Math.log(2)*(f/fw)**2), span = 1.6*fw, curve = [], modes = [];
  for (let i = 0; i &lt;= 400; i++) { const f = -span + 2*span*i/400; curve.push({f: f/1e9, g: G(f)}); }
  const m0 = Math.round(-span/dnm) - 1, m1 = Math.round(span/dnm) + 1;
  if (m1 - m0 &lt; 4000) for (let m = m0; m &lt;= m1; m++) { const f = (m + gl.shift)*dnm; if (Math.abs(f) &lt;= span) modes.push({f: f/1e9, g: G(f), lase: G(f) &gt; gl.thr}); }
  const nLase = modes.filter(d =&gt; d.lase).length;
  return {fw, dnm, curve, modes, nLase, lam0, nu0, dlam: fw*lam0/nu0, mnum: 2*gl.L*1e-2/(lam0*1e-9)};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.min(width, 1050), height: 300, x: {label: "frequency offset ν − ν₀ (GHz)"}, y: {label: "gain / peak gain", domain: [0, 1.05], grid: true},
  marks: [Plot.areaY(glOut.curve, {x: "f", y: "g", fill: "#eb6834", fillOpacity: 0.15}), Plot.line(glOut.curve, {x: "f", y: "g", stroke: "#eb6834", strokeWidth: 2}),
          Plot.ruleX(glOut.modes, {x: "f", y1: 0, y2: "g", stroke: d =&gt; d.lase ? "#2a78d6" : "#c3c2b7", strokeWidth: glOut.modes.length &gt; 200 ? 1 : 2.5}),
          Plot.ruleY([gl.thr], {stroke: "#e34948", strokeDasharray: "5,3"})]
})


















Show the Python
html`&lt;div class="result-box"&gt;Doppler FWHM Δν&lt;sub&gt;1/2&lt;/sub&gt; = &lt;b&gt;${(glOut.fw/1e9).toFixed(3)} GHz&lt;/b&gt; (Δλ&lt;sub&gt;1/2&lt;/sub&gt; = ${(glOut.dlam*1e3).toPrecision(3)} pm) ·
mode spacing c/2L = &lt;b&gt;${(glOut.dnm/1e6).toFixed(1)} MHz&lt;/b&gt; · mode number m ≈ ${glOut.mnum.toExponential(4)} ·
modes above threshold: &lt;b&gt;${glOut.nLase}&lt;/b&gt; (estimate Δν&lt;sub&gt;1/2&lt;/sub&gt;/Δν&lt;sub&gt;m&lt;/sub&gt; = ${(glOut.fw/glOut.dnm).toFixed(1)})&lt;/div&gt;`







Two consequences show up immediately:

The number of lasing modes depends on where the comb sits under the gain curve (“mode position”). Moving the comb by half a spacing changes the count by one. In a real laser the cavity length drifts thermally by many wavelengths, so the modes slide under the curve and the output power fluctuates (“mode sweeping”).
A cavity shorter than about \(c/(2\Delta\nu_{1/2})\approx10\) cm keeps a single He–Ne mode above threshold. Single-frequency He–Ne lasers are short, and their output is low.

6.2 Threshold calculator


Show the Python
presets = ({
  "He–Ne (slides 69, 73)":        {L: 40, R1: 0.95, R2: 1.0, as: 0.05, n: 1.0, lam: 632.8, dnu: 1.5e9, tsp: 100e-9, d: 0.8},
  "Nd:YAG rod":                   {L: 10, R1: 0.90, R2: 0.999, as: 0.3, n: 1.82, lam: 1064, dnu: 120e9, tsp: 230e-6, d: 4},
  "GaAs laser diode (slide 131)": {L: 0.025, R1: 0.32, R2: 0.32, as: 1000, n: 3.6, lam: 860, dnu: 8e12, tsp: 2.5e-9, d: 0.002},
  "InGaAsP FP diode, one HR facet":{L: 0.03, R1: 0.30, R2: 0.90, as: 1500, n: 3.5, lam: 1550, dnu: 5e12, tsp: 2e-9, d: 0.002}
})
viewof preset = Inputs.select(Object.keys(presets), {label: "start from"})


















Show the Python
viewof th = {
  const p = presets[preset];
  return Inputs.form({
    L: Inputs.number({value: p.L, label: "cavity length L (cm)"}),
    R1: Inputs.range([0.01, 0.9999], {value: p.R1, step: 0.0001, label: "output mirror R₁"}),
    R2: Inputs.range([0.01, 1], {value: p.R2, step: 0.0001, label: "back mirror R₂"}),
    as: Inputs.number({value: p.as, label: "internal loss α_s (m⁻¹)"}),
    n: Inputs.number({value: p.n, label: "refractive index n"}),
    lam: Inputs.number({value: p.lam, label: "wavelength λ₀ (nm)"}),
    dnu: Inputs.number({value: p.dnu, label: "gain linewidth Δν (Hz)"}),
    tsp: Inputs.number({value: p.tsp, label: "spontaneous lifetime τ_sp (s)"})
  });
}









Show the Python
thOut = {
  const c = 299792458, L = th.L*1e-2, nu = c/(th.lam*1e-9);
  const mir = Math.log(1/(th.R1*th.R2))/(2*L), at = th.as + mir;
  const tph = 2*th.n*L/(c*(1 - Math.exp(-2*at*L))), tph0 = th.n/(c*at);
  const dN = at*8*Math.PI*th.n**2*nu**2*th.tsp*th.dnu/c**2;
  const fsr = c/(2*th.n*L), dnuc = 1/(2*Math.PI*tph), Q = 2*Math.PI*nu*tph;
  return {at, mir, tph, tph0, dN, fsr, dnuc, Q, F: fsr/dnuc, ee: Math.log(1/th.R1)/(2*L)/at};
}
html`&lt;div class="result-box"&gt;
threshold gain g&lt;sub&gt;th&lt;/sub&gt; = α&lt;sub&gt;t&lt;/sub&gt; = &lt;b&gt;${thOut.at.toPrecision(4)} m⁻¹&lt;/b&gt; (${(thOut.at/100).toPrecision(3)} cm⁻¹; mirrors ${(100*thOut.mir/thOut.at).toFixed(0)} %, internal ${(100*th.as/thOut.at).toFixed(0)} %)&lt;br&gt;
threshold inversion (N₂ − N₁)&lt;sub&gt;th&lt;/sub&gt; = &lt;b&gt;${thOut.dN.toExponential(3)} m⁻³&lt;/b&gt; (${(thOut.dN/1e6).toExponential(2)} cm⁻³)&lt;br&gt;
photon lifetime τ&lt;sub&gt;ph&lt;/sub&gt; = &lt;b&gt;${thOut.tph &lt; 1e-9 ? (thOut.tph*1e12).toFixed(2) + " ps" : (thOut.tph*1e9).toFixed(2) + " ns"}&lt;/b&gt; (approximation n/cα&lt;sub&gt;t&lt;/sub&gt;: ${thOut.tph0 &lt; 1e-9 ? (thOut.tph0*1e12).toFixed(2) + " ps" : (thOut.tph0*1e9).toFixed(2) + " ns"})&lt;br&gt;
cold-cavity linewidth 1/2πτ&lt;sub&gt;ph&lt;/sub&gt; = ${(thOut.dnuc/1e6).toPrecision(3)} MHz · mode spacing c/2nL = ${(thOut.fsr/1e9).toPrecision(4)} GHz · finesse ≈ ${thOut.F.toPrecision(3)} · Q = ${thOut.Q.toExponential(2)}&lt;br&gt;
fraction of the loss leaving through R₁ (extraction efficiency): ${(100*thOut.ee).toFixed(1)} %&lt;/div&gt;`
















Compare the presets. The He–Ne laser has a tiny gain (0.1 m⁻¹) and needs high-reflectivity mirrors. The laser diode has an enormous loss (55 cm⁻¹), mostly through its 32 % cleaved facets, and compensates with an enormous gain. Its photon lifetime is picoseconds, which is what makes GHz modulation possible (Chapter 16).

7.2 Transverse modes (slide 75)


Show the Python
viewof tm = Inputs.form({
  m: Inputs.range([0, 6], {value: 1, step: 1, label: "m (nodes along x)"}),
  n: Inputs.range([0, 6], {value: 0, step: 1, label: "n (nodes along y)"}),
  donut: Inputs.toggle({label: "add the rotated mode with a 90° phase (TEM₀₁* 'donut')", value: false})
})









Show the Python
tmOut = {
  const H = (k, x) =&gt; { let h0 = 1, h1 = 2*x; if (k === 0) return h0; for (let i = 1; i &lt; k; i++) { const h2 = 2*x*h1 - 2*i*h0; h0 = h1; h1 = h2; } return h1; };
  const N = 121, ext = 3.2, v = new Float64Array(N*N);
  for (let j = 0; j &lt; N; j++) for (let i = 0; i &lt; N; i++) {
    const x = -ext + 2*ext*i/(N - 1), y = -ext + 2*ext*j/(N - 1), g = Math.exp(-(x*x + y*y));
    const a = H(tm.m, Math.SQRT2*x)*H(tm.n, Math.SQRT2*y)*g, b = H(tm.n, Math.SQRT2*x)*H(tm.m, Math.SQRT2*y)*g;
    v[j*N + i] = tm.donut ? a*a + b*b : a*a;
  }
  const mx = d3.max(v); for (let k = 0; k &lt; v.length; k++) v[k] /= mx;
  return {v, N, ext};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.min(width, 420), height: Math.min(width, 420), aspectRatio: 1,
  x: {label: "x/w", domain: [-tmOut.ext, tmOut.ext]}, y: {label: "y/w", domain: [-tmOut.ext, tmOut.ext]},
  color: {scheme: "magma"},
  marks: [Plot.raster(tmOut.v, {width: tmOut.N, height: tmOut.N, x1: -tmOut.ext, x2: tmOut.ext, y1: -tmOut.ext, y2: tmOut.ext, interpolate: "nearest"})]
})
















Each transverse mode \((m,n)\) has its own set of longitudinal resonances, shifted from those of TEM\(_{00}\) by the Gouy phase. A laser that oscillates on several transverse modes therefore emits extra frequencies and a larger, irregular beam. The frequencies and the mode size follow from the mirror curvatures: see Going further: optical resonators. An aperture inside the cavity (or, in a laser diode, a narrow stripe, Chapter 10) adds loss to the wider high-order modes and keeps only TEM\(_{00}\).

7.4 Spectral hole burning (slide 82)
A strong beam at \(\nu_s\) saturates the atoms it interacts with. In a homogeneous medium every atom sees the beam, so the whole gain curve drops. In an inhomogeneous medium only the atoms whose own (homogeneous) line overlaps \(\nu_s\) are saturated: a hole appears in the gain curve, of width \(\Delta\nu_h\sqrt{1+I/I_\text{sat}}\).


Show the Python
viewof hb = Inputs.form({
  hom: Inputs.range([0.02, 3], {value: 0.15, step: 0.01, label: "homogeneous FWHM / inhomogeneous FWHM"}),
  I: Inputs.range([0, 50], {value: 5, step: 0.5, label: "saturating intensity I/I_sat"}),
  nus: Inputs.range([-1.5, 1.5], {value: 0.3, step: 0.01, label: "saturating frequency (units of inhomogeneous FWHM)"})
})









Show the Python
hbOut = {
  const G = 1/(2*Math.sqrt(Math.LN2)), gam = hb.hom/2, Lr = d =&gt; gam*gam/(d*d + gam*gam);      // L normalised to 1 at centre
  const N = 401, data = [], cls = d3.range(N).map(i =&gt; -2.5 + 5*i/(N - 1)), w = cls.map(c =&gt; Math.exp(-(c*c)/(G*G)));
  let norm = 0; for (let k = 0; k &lt; N; k++) norm += w[k]*Lr(cls[k]); // unsaturated gain at line centre, for normalisation
  for (let i = 0; i &lt;= 300; i++) {
    const nu = -2 + 4*i/300; let g0 = 0, g = 0;
    for (let k = 0; k &lt; N; k++) { const l = w[k]*Lr(nu - cls[k]); g0 += l; g += l/(1 + hb.I*Lr(hb.nus - cls[k])); }
    data.push({nu, g: g0/norm, who: "unsaturated"}, {nu, g: g/norm, who: "with saturating beam"});
  }
  return {data};
}
Plot.plot({
  width: Math.min(width, 1050), height: 280, x: {label: "ν − ν₀ (units of the inhomogeneous FWHM)"}, y: {label: "gain (normalised)", grid: true},
  color: {legend: true, range: ["#c3c2b7", "#2a78d6"]},
  marks: [Plot.line(hbOut.data, {x: "nu", y: "g", stroke: "who", strokeWidth: 2}), Plot.ruleX([hb.nus], {stroke: "#e34948", strokeDasharray: "4,3"})]
})
















Make the homogeneous width larger than the inhomogeneous one: the hole widens until the whole curve sinks uniformly. This is the key difference of slide 83. In a homogeneous medium the first mode to reach threshold clamps the gain everywhere and the others stay below threshold: single-mode operation (Nd:YAG, in principle). In an inhomogeneous medium each mode burns its own hole and several modes lase at once (He–Ne). In a He–Ne laser each mode burns two holes, at \(\nu_m\) and \(2\nu_0-\nu_m\), because the standing wave interacts with atoms moving in both directions. Spatial hole burning in the standing wave breaks the single-mode rule of homogeneous media in practice.






TipGoing further






Lifetime broadening from a Fourier transform. Sample \(E(t)=e^{-t/2\tau}\cos\omega_0t\) for \(t\ge0\), take np.fft.fft and fit a Lorentzian to \(|E(\nu)|^2\). Check that the FWHM is \(1/2\pi\tau\).
Spatial hole burning. In a standing-wave cavity the intensity has nodes every \(\lambda/2n\). Atoms at the nodes are not saturated by the lasing mode and provide gain for a second mode. This is why Nd:YAG lasers are multimode unless they use a ring cavity or a twisted-mode cavity.
Inhomogeneous broadening in semiconductors? The gain of a bulk semiconductor is homogeneously broadened by fast intraband scattering (0.1 ps). Quantum-dot lasers, with their size distribution, are strongly inhomogeneous: they lase on many modes and are used for frequency combs.










